 of debris combinations of  broken pottery objects people dropped  animal dung that's been sort of shoveled  up into these mounds to keep it from say  blowing around where people are working  and living so clearing through that  material sifting through it wanna fuying  the material very often we have groups  of graduate students coming out at one  of the sites that do now at your site  and harga this requires sorting of  fabrics so different types of pots may  be made out of different types of clays  and if you can count them up and weigh  them you can quantify how many bowls and  what weight of bowls how many jars and  what weight of jars and what types of  fabrics and it can be very instructive  than to compare this quantified data  between the levels because you can say  sometimes by doing that Wow something  really changed at this period totally  different types of pots different ratios  and even my words your site what we  found was that it was remarkably uniform  what they were doing and how they were  doing it so the the baking process let's  say  this one site we realized was done in a  really I guess you could say almost  automated fashion like same type spots  in the same ratios same types of clays  in the same ratios doing the same thing  over about a century so some sites  they're just a couple of us working on  wrapping up copying rock inscriptions  other times we may have a dozen students  and associated people like paleobotanist  at cetera with us and we also have a  permanent staff of Egyptian workmen  who've worked with us now for years and  who really understand the excavation  techniques that you need in the desert  one of the weird things in the desert by  the way is you know if you're digging in  soil if you're in the Nile Valley you're  going through a matrix trying to find  the objects in that matrix in the desert  at these activity sites these living  sides these caravans arrives there's no  soil so the entire matrix is also  archaeological material so it's a  completely different sort of technique  that you're not looking for objects in a  matrix you're actually going through I  don't know how you'd call it like an  artifact xual matrix trying to quantify  the material trying to see how the site  form that sort of thing it must take  forever I mean is it a very lengthy  process  is it a very lengthy process I mean are  you working on a project for years and  years or I would imagine it does vary  depending on where you are but it seems  like it would take forever to like clear  the sand off of something for instance  it can it can take a tremendous amount  of time one of our interesting  discoveries at a site called tomb dhaba  between the Nile Valley and harga Oasis  was a cistern that had been dug around  in its final form around 1550 1600 BCE  it was about a hundred feet deep dug  through initially an upper level of sort  of fossil alluvial soil that had filled  eight  in remote antiquity and the high desert  plateau they dug through that a big  spiral staircase and then a shaft in two  major segments down through the  limestone to gather rainwater that would  fall in this depression it was about a  hundred feet deep and it really was very  difficult to to clear we could only stay  on site for about a month at a time  before it really became rather difficult  to keep the vehicles and repair and  because they have to keep going back and  forth to bring in supplies we would dig  it as deep clear it as deeply as we  could of sand and continuing to work on  the surrounding area come back the next  year and about three to four meters of  sand would fill in that in the  intervening time so you'd go down sand  would build up a little bit go back down  sand would build up a little bit that's  a problem at a lot of the side so what  we have to do is sort of triage clearing  of random areas in a larger site is is a  necessary initial process anyway so that  you can be absolutely certain that  you're really getting a good snapshot  that that you're not just letting your  eyes be attracted and say that's the  highest part I just I'm just going to  concentrate in the highest part you  really have to try to be a combination  of selectively going for areas that you  think probably would be important and at  the same time making sure that you're  picking randomly in your grid certain  areas to clear and usually after a few  seasons you you you get an idea as to  whether you've gotten most of the data  one of the things that I have heard  about and read is that there are thieves  and vandals have you experienced that  and since you do go back repeatedly year  after year to sites have you had any you  know heartbreak in that area for  instance we do encounter vandals we do  encounter or land reclamation even in  some of the more remote parts of the  desert it is very sad depressing thing  to have  and one of the sites of which we've  worked for a number of seasons the  waterhole was being vandalized when we  first got to the site thieves were  already attacking the site we've done  our best to try to protect the area but  as in most things it's easier to go out  and to destroy something than it is to  record it and one of the very important  inscriptions in the audio hole a letter  that was carved by a priest addressed to  another priest but carved on the rock  face so probably the only sort of  monumental lapidary spontaneous letter  like that from ancient Egypt that also  quoted from the story of sinawe this is  also one of actually I forget about this  always this is actually a rather  interesting discovery it's just a person  traveling this road he's a priest he's  writing to another priest who had left  his inscription close by so it's this  kind of joke in a way I guess he knows  this man's going to combine he'll see  his letter but in his letter he quotes  from the story of sinawe a literary text  so it shows not only that this man that  who so back had read story of si